---
import Layout from "#/ui/layouts/Layout.astro";
import GitHubIcon from "#/assets/github-original.svg";
---

<Layout
  title="ErrorScript - Typed Errors in TypeScript"
  description="Typed errors in TypeScript without verbosity. ErrorScript makes failure part of the conversation, so you can write code with confidence."
>
  <div class="page">
    <main class="landing">
      <section class="section hero">
        <header class="heading">
          <h1>ErrorScript</h1>

          <p class="subheadline">Typed Errors in TypeScript</p>
        </header>

        <p class="description">
          Explicit error handling in TypeScript without verbosity and
          complexity.
        </p>

        <nav class="links">
          <ul>
            <li>
              <a href="https://github.com/kossnocorp/errorscript" class="link">
                <GitHubIcon class="icon" />GitHub
              </a>
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </section>

      <section class="section why">
        <h2><i>Effective</i> Error Handling</h2>

        <p>
          <a href="https://effect.website/">Effect</a> is great. t not only gives
          missing errors-as-values but also provides production-ready architecture.
        </p>

        <p>
          It deserves all the love and praise, but it is undeniable that it
          forces you to adopt it entirely if you want to benefit from it and
          trade simplicity for robustness.
        </p>

        <p>
          And that's the price you have to pay to get typed errors. Or have you?
        </p>

        <p>
          ErrorScript offers a simpler alternative that utilizes the blazing
          fast Oxc compiler and statically analyzes every function call and
          figures out the exact errors it can throw.
        </p>

        <p>
          It sounds impossible, and there's a reason why nobody has achieved it
          so far, but it actually can be done. In fact, I did it.
        </p>

        <p>
          Currently ErrorScript is in the prototype stage, but it is already
          apparent that it will work. All that's left is a ton of work, but
          nowadays it's not a problem, right?
        </p>

        <p>
          Interested? <a href="https://x.com/kossnocorp" class="link"
            >Contact me on X</a
          >.
        </p>

        <p><i>— <a href="https://koss.nocorp.me" class="link">Sasha</a></i></p>
      </section>
    </main>

    <footer>
      Sasha Koss © 2026 <a href="https://koss.nocorp.me/mit" class="link"
        >MIT License</a
      >
    </footer>
  </div>
</Layout>

<style>
  @reference "#/ui/styles/global.css";

  .page {
    @apply w-full max-w-5xl px-6 py-12 sm:px-12 sm:py-20 flex flex-col gap-20;
  }

  .landing {
    @apply flex flex-col gap-12;
  }

  .section {
    @apply flex flex-col gap-4;
  }

  .why {
    @apply prose dark:prose-invert gap-5 max-w-xl;

    /* Let the flex gap control spacing instead of prose's block margins. */
    & > :where(*) {
      @apply my-0;
    }

    & > :where(h2, h3, h4, h5, h6):not(:first-child) {
      @apply mt-4;
    }
  }

  h1 {
    @apply text-5xl sm:text-7xl font-bold text-(--color-heading-1);
  }

  h2 {
    @apply text-xl font-bold text-(--color-heading-2);
  }

  h3 {
    @apply text-base font-bold text-(--color-heading-3);
  }

  .hero {
    & .heading {
      @apply flex flex-col gap-2;

      & .subheadline {
        @apply text-xl font-semibold text-(--color-heading-2);
      }
    }

    & .description,
    & .links {
      @apply text-balance max-w-xl;
    }

    & .description {
      @apply text-(--color-text) text-lg;
    }
  }

  .points {
    @apply flex flex-col gap-2 text-balance max-w-xl;

    & dt,
    & dd {
      @apply my-0;
    }

    & dd + dt {
      @apply mt-4;
    }

    & dt {
      @apply font-semibold text-(--color-heading-2);
    }

    & dd {
      @apply ms-0 text-base text-balance text-(--color-text);
    }
  }

  footer {
    @apply opacity-70 hover:opacity-100;
  }
</style>
